# 098-engine-surface.syms — what code arriving after the build may call.
#
# What this is: the list of engine symbols kept in the executable's
# dynamic symbol table. A box or a map compiled while the program runs
# arrives as a shared object and is opened with dlopen; a shared object
# can only call a symbol the host executable exported. This file is
# that export list, and so it is the engine's public surface written
# down — the first time that boundary has existed anywhere except by
# accident.
#
# How it does it, in general terms: the linker is handed this file as
# --dynamic-list. Every name matching a pattern here goes into the
# dynamic table and becomes a root the section collector has to keep.
# Everything else is fair game for --gc-sections to discard.
#
# Why this is not -rdynamic (issue 311d). -rdynamic exports *every*
# global symbol, and an exported symbol is a collection root by
# definition: the whole point of exporting it is that code which does
# not exist yet may look it up by name, so the linker can prove nothing
# about who calls it and must keep it. -rdynamic therefore declares the
# entire binary reachable, and --gc-sections then collects nothing. The
# two flags are in direct opposition and -rdynamic wins.
#
# Measured on a five-function model before this file was written: a
# program placing one box out of three kept one shim under
# --gc-sections alone, and all three shims plus all three placement
# functions once -rdynamic was added. With this file it keeps one and
# still exports the engine.
#
# That is what makes "a program using three boxes out of five hundred
# does not carry five hundred shims" true without the generator having
# to guess which three. The linker traces reachability through
# hand-written extern declarations and through function pointers taken
# by name — the two cases a source parser gets wrong.
#
# A symbol listed here survives collection even when nothing compiled
# into the binary calls it, which is the property the whole idea rests
# on: a box arriving mid-run calls engine functions that this
# particular program may never call itself.
#
# Why prefixes rather than a list of names. Three families of function
# can be reached from code that arrives late, and naming the families
# says which layers are public without a list that has to be re-edited
# every time one of them gains a function. The narrower alternative —
# naming the individual functions generated code actually calls, which
# today is far fewer — is recorded as an open question on 311d rather
# than taken, because a list that breaks loudly on every addition is
# only worth having once the surface has stopped moving.
#
# Leaving a family out is not a silent loss: a shared object that needs
# a symbol the host did not export fails at dlopen, naming the symbol.

{
    # The construction surface. A placement function calls into it to
    # write a station; a compiled map calls it to build a whole
    # program; the construction boxes call it on behalf of a map that
    # is building another map.
    map_*;

    # Stopping. A box may end the program it is running inside, which
    # is the one thing a box does that is not pure computation.
    sora_*;
};
